<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">
  <assemblyIdentity version="1.0.0.0" name="LeagueAccountManager.app" />

  <!--
    requireAdministrator, reluctantly, because the app has to match the Riot Client's integrity level.

    RiotClientServices.exe ships an asInvoker manifest, so in principle nothing here needs elevation.
    In practice a machine can carry a "Run this program as an administrator" compatibility layer for
    it (HKCU\...\AppCompatFlags\Layers -> RUNASADMIN), which League troubleshooting guides hand out
    freely. When that layer is set the client runs at high integrity, and Windows UIPI then forbids a
    medium-integrity process from doing all three things this app needs:

      * sending it keystrokes  (autofill)
      * reading its UI Automation tree  (finding the login fields)
      * closing it  (every account switch begins by shutting the client down)

    Running at the same level is the only way to keep those working without modifying the user's Riot
    setup. Elevation does not change the user profile, so the vault stays at
    %APPDATA%\LeagueAccountManager and needs no migration.

    The cost is a UAC prompt on every launch, and a process holding decrypted secrets running with
    more privilege than it would otherwise need. If the RUNASADMIN layer is ever cleared from the
    Riot executables, this can and should go back to asInvoker.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v2">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false" />
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" /><!-- Win 10/11 -->
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/PM</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
      <longPathAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">true</longPathAware>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>
</assembly>
